```javascript
// Plain event-driven: imperative state tracking inside the handler
let lastClickTime = 0;
button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  const now = Date.now();
  if (now - lastClickTime > 300) {   // manual debounce logic
    handleClick();
  }
  lastClickTime = now;
});

// Reactive: the same debounce expressed as a stream operator
fromEvent(button, 'click')
  .pipe(debounceTime(300))
  .subscribe(() => handleClick());
```

Both versions do the same thing — ignore clicks that arrive within 300ms of the last one — but the imperative version hand-rolls timing state (`lastClickTime`) inside the handler, which is easy to get subtly wrong (off-by-one on the comparison, forgetting to update the timestamp in every code path). The reactive version delegates the timing logic entirely to the `debounceTime` operator, which is written once, tested once, and reused across every stream in the codebase that needs the same behavior.

The reactive version also composes: adding a `.filter()` or a `.map()` before `.subscribe()` doesn't require touching any hand-rolled state at all.
